hen it's most  likely that we'll get a relatively large  p-value and we won't reject the null  hypothesis that's the whole way that  hypothesis tests are constructed but now  let's imagine  that instead of just flipping one coin  10 times we somehow find under the sofa  1024  fair coins at this point it's very clear  that this is just a thought experiment  you haven't seen my sofa but  so then we flip this coin each one of  these coins 10 times so we now  this could take a while so we'll edit  that part out but  if we do that  why did we choose 1024 as the number  here daniella  i think it's because that's 2 to the  10th gareth  and so the probability that if we flip  one coin  10 times that we get 10 tails  is  exactly 1 over one thousand and twenty  four one over ten to the uh two to the  ten  so if we flip that many coins ten times  then on average we would expect to get  about one coin where it came tails every  single time and if we were to perform a  hypothesis test on that one particular  coin  we'd get a p value of about point zero  zero two so a very small p value and why  is it point zero zero two carats  because the probability of a coin coming  up tails 10 times in a row is only 0.002  or coming up heads uh 10 times in a row  so right because it's 1 over 10 24 plus  1 over 10 24. there's two ways that we  could get this extreme an outcome  because we would be really surprised if  we think that the coin's probably fair  and that's our null hypothesis we'll be  really surprised if we get 10 tails we'd  be equally surprised to get 10 heads  presumably so that's why to get the  p-value it's 1 over 10 24 plus 1 over 10  24. because we're not specifying that  it's more likely to be tails or heads  under the alternative it's just not  equal  so  if we were to  look at that one particular coin and do  the hypothesis test on that coin it's  almost certain that we would end up  rejecting the null hypothesis  the other coins maybe we don't reject  but the point here is that if we perform  enough hypothesis tests even when we  insist on a very small p-value it's  almost certain that we'll make some in  this case type 1 mistakes this would be  a type 1 mistake because these coins are  in fact fair the null hypothesis is true  but at least for that particular coin  we've incorrectly rejected it  and this slide here i think this cartoon  sort of illustrates this idea pretty  nicely so i'm not sure if you can read  this on the screen but the concept here  is that they perform a hypothesis test  on jelly beans to check if there's a  relationship between jelly beans and  acne  and of course there is no relationship  between jelly beans  i mean we should probably do the  experiment guess  i don't like jelly beans so i'm not  gonna do it but if you want to you can  try but in this particular cartoon at  least they found no relationship they  had a large p value but then the claim  was well maybe it's not jelly beans in  particular it's particular colors of  jelly beans so  you notice here that we have exactly 20  of these cartoons corresponding to 20  different colors in the rectangular big  box  and that color corresponds to 20 colors  and for 19 of those colors they also  failed to reject a null hypothesis with  a p value of 5 but it turned out that  the green jelly beans had a p-value  below five percent  and so you see suddenly here that  there's a newspaper story that green  jelly beans are linked to acne  but the reality is we just performed a  large number of hypothesis tests  probably the null hypothesis was true in  every one of them  but if we do enough we're almost  guaranteed to get one false positive  and so this relates to all the  reproducibility issues and things as  well that  happen these days  and it also relates to the idea that if  you want to either sound really smart or  be really annoying potato potato then  whenever you see a headline about any  kind of scientific or social science  study or anything like that you can just  be like  i think they had a multiple testing  problem and like you're number one  you're probably right but also very  annoying yeah statisticians don't get  invited to a lot of parties  that is definitely but there's probably  more than one reason for that that's  true yeah  okay so this just sort of illustrates  the challenge that we have in doing a  hypothesis test that if we test this  large number of hypotheses all of which  are true we reject any null hypothesis  with a p-value below say one percent  then we're going to have approximately  0.01 times m whatever the value of m is  null hypotheses that we falsely reject  so that's type one errors we're going to  have .01 times m type one errors if all  of the null hypotheses were true so for  example if we had 10 000 tests and all  of the null hypotheses were true then  we'd expect to falsely reject about 100  null hypotheses by chance so suddenly  there's a hundred newspaper articles  about these fantastic discoveries at a  very low p-value when the reality is we  just performed too many hypothesis tests  or at least without correcting for that  issue so a lot of type 1 errors false  positives  all right so then what's sort of an  approach that we can take to try to deal  with this situation we just discussed  that if we perform enough of these  hypothesis tests we're almost certain to  have some type 1 errors we don't want  type 1 errors so  how can we try to control for the  situation where we're doing a lot of  these tests and the sort of most  classical approach here is something  called the family-wise error rate  and the family-wise zero rate is just  defined as the probability that we make  at least one type one error so if you  think about it if you have a low  family-wise error rate then you're  saying that there's a low probability of  making any type 1 errors sounds great  right so this table here is just sort of  an analogous table to daniella's one  that she showed us earlier this again  has sort of the four possible situations  the two possible real states of the  world and the two possible decisions  that we could make here  and the letters here just represent if i  do m of these hypothesis tests how many  of those tests fall into each of these  four buckets and so for example the  bucket that we're really concerned about  here is the one where h0 is true but we  reject h.i that's the type one error  each night he means each night  h o is true  so  v here the the letter v here just  represents the number of type one errors  that we're making out of m hypothesis  here so the family y zero right here is  by definition the probability that v is  greater than one  so to be clear we we know how many null  hypotheses we've rejected and we know  how many we have and rejected so we know  r and we know m minus r and of course we  know m which is the number of hypothesis  tests we've conducted but we don't know  which column what the values in the  columns are so we don't know the value  of v we don't know the value of  s and so on  right but we can in general calculate or  at least approximate the probability  that v is greater than one or this  family y zero rate or we can hope to try  to control what that value is and that's  what we're going to talk about  now are some approaches for trying to  control that family-wise error rate and  just before we go onto that i just want  to say gareth in your courtroom analogy  i think that the idea here is we have m  defendants each of whom is being put on  trial  and  v corresponds to  finding one of the defendants guilty  when in fact none of them are guilty and  we would be really presumably we would  not want to find even one of the m  defendants guilty if in fact they're all  innocent so that's why we're stressed  about the possibility of v being greater  than or equal to one we want that  family-wise error rate the probability  of wrongfully convicting someone to be  very very small wrongly convicting  anyone among the m to be very small what  how does it go better to let 10 innocent  people go  10 guilty people go than to convict one  innocent person so although here they're  all innocent because the family-wise  error rate we're thinking about the null  hypothesis all being true right  okay so controlling the family y zero  rate sounds like a good thing if we have  a small family y zero rate we're likely  to make no type one errors but in  practice it's often hard to actually  control that and and this formula here  sort of illustrates how you might  calculate the family-wise error rate  and what it's really saying is that it's  equal to one minus the probability of  the intersection that we do not falsely  reject the jth null hypothesis  now in practice that probability is hard  to  calculate in a general situation but if  we simplify things and imagine that each  of our hypotheses tests are independent  so that  intersection becomes a product of the  individual probabilities and we assume  that all the null hypotheses are true  and we only reject if the p values less  than alpha then it's not too hard to  show that that family y zero rate is  equal to this one minus alpha to the m  quantity  and so what does that look like for  different values of m and different  values of alpha so this plot here just  shows for three different values of  alpha five percent one percent and point  zero one percent and different values of  m what happens to that family y zero  rate and the dotted line down the bottom  is is at the five percent level so if  you wanted to have a family y zero rate  of five percent what value of m is going  to work for that and  basically what this shows you is that  when m gets even moderately large you  can end up with family-wise error rates  that are close to 100 so  in that sense the family-wise error rate  is not helping you at all because you're  almost certain to make at least one type  1 error here so the way we read this  plot is if you look at the purple curve  like when m equals 50 so when you're at  50 on the x-axis  the purple curve is right around 0.05  and so that means if you reject any null  hypothesis if there p if it's p-value is  below 0.01 but you have 50 null  hypotheses then there's a five percent  chance of making a family wise  error a type one error type one  at least one type one error but you can  see that once you get up to say 500 here  even at that very very low value of  alpha you're already up to about a 40  chance of making at least one type one  error so this family-wise error rate is  essentially a very hard quantity to  control for very large values of m and  the only way to control it is to have  your alpha be tiny and remember alpha is  the type one error that you're trying to  control so it's like the threshold at  which you're going to reject the null  hypothesis if you see a p-value less  than that alpha

